`;function gie(){return T.jsxs("div",{className:"col-start-3 row-start-3 p-8 text-left",children:[T.jsxs("div",{className:"max-w-5xl mx-auto",children:[T.jsx("div",{className:"text-center text-sm font-medium text-gray-400 tracking-wide mb-3",children:"ELITE / Blog /"}),T.jsx("h1",{className:"text-center text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-wide uppercase text-slate-900",children:"Tech Unpacked: From Real-Life Objects to 3D Digital Twins"})]}),T.jsx("div",{className:"mt-10 -mx-8",children:T.jsx("img",{src:"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1752588975228-21f44630bb3c?q=80&w=1200&auto=format&fit=crop",alt:"Full body workout",className:"w-full h-[420px] md:h-[520px] object-cover"})}),T.jsxs("div",{className:"max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-12 mb-20",children:[T.jsxs("div",{className:"flex justify-between items-center text-gray-600 text-sm mb-10",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"AUTHOR"}),T.jsx("span",{className:"uppercase tracking-wide",children:"6 January 2025"})]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Introduction "}),T.jsx("br",{}),"As educators, we are often required to illustrate abstract ideas for our students. In fields like biology, history, or art, using physical objects can facilitate the linkage between theory and the real world. Unfortunately, time limits or practical issues can sometimes get in the way. Have you ever wanted to pass around an object in class, only to find there is not enough time for everyone to see it up close? Or maybe it is too valuable or fragile that even bringing it to class will be risky? Or have you spotted an interesting structure outside the classroom that could support your teaching, but struggled to show all its details using just photos?"]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Why CAVE?"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"According to the"," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/virtual-reality",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"Cambridge Dictionary"}),', VR refers to "a set of images and sounds, produced by a computer, that seem to represent a place or a situation that a person can take part in”. Ultimately, it is a digital system designed to maximize user immersion.',T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("br",{}),"Typically, people experience VR by wearing specialized headsets, known as head-mounted displays (HMDs), such as those from popular brands like HTC VIVE, Meta Quest, or PICO. However, this form of VR is only optimized for individual experience, where the user is isolated from the outside world and surrounding people. Of course, through streaming the display, other people can see what is unfolding in the virtual world, but their roles remain merely spectators instead of active participants. In a classroom setting, this can lead to lowered group engagement as a small number of students are submerged in the experience while the others can only stand and watch from the outside world. A solution to this is the CAVE system, which stands for Cave Automatic Virtual Environment. Developed in the 1990s and continuing to evolve, CAVE provides an alternative way to experience VR without isolating the user from the real world."]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"The Setup"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"A CAVE system consists of two main components: display and tracking. The display component uses screens that surround the user, such as in a cubic or circular form. These screens project images that immerse the user in virtual environments, like a historical site or a medical facility. Additionally, the screens can display stereoscopic 3D graphics, providing a sense of depth and distance when paired with active shutter 3D glasses. The tracking component is essential for both viewing and interaction. It consists of sensors to track the user's head position, ensuring the images adjust to provide the correct perspective as the user moves. It can also track body parts or real-life objects, such as tools or equipment, allowing them to be utilized for interaction with the virtual scene."]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Benefits "}),T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"1. Shared experience"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"One major issue with HMD-based VR is isolation. CAVE solves this by using a shared display with multiple users looking at the same images, synchronizing their visual experience. More importantly, the users are able to see one another, making communication and coordination much easier. This shared experience makes the system ideal for group activities in education.",T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"2. Natural movement and interaction"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"Movement and interaction in CAVE also feel more natural since users wear only lightweight items like 3D glasses and trackers, avoiding bulky headsets or cables. This reduces intrusion and boosts acceptance among students who might find headsets uncomfortable. The risk of bumping into objects is reduced too, as the surroundings are not blocked by the headset.",T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"3. Integration of real-life objects"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"Another key advantage is the capability of incorporating real-life objects into the virtual environment. For example, a fire extinguisher with a tracker attached can be used directly as an in-game tool, making interactions more realistic and intuitive. This is especially beneficial for simulated training that emphasizes resemblance of real-life actions, like medical procedures and driving. Also, it feels more natural and engaging for users to be able to handle familiar objects instead of controllers or gesturing in the air. This utilization of physical objects in a virtual scene is not as straightforward when using headsets.",T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("br",{}),"This raises an interesting question as well: Is CAVE a form of augmented virtuality (a mixed reality concept that brings real-world elements into a predominantly virtual space)?",T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"4. Easier guidance"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"An often-overlooked benefit of CAVE, especially in education, is the ease of guiding users. In HMD-based VR, it is sometimes challenging to instruct users because they cannot see the real world, including you. Therefore, instead of just gesturing or pointing at buttons and objects in the virtual scene directly, we often need to describe visual elements verbally. Sometimes, we may even need to tell the user to take off the headset just for more effective communication. In contrast, CAVE makes guidance much easier, allowing for direct and natural communication between the users and the instructor as they are in the same space, both virtually and physically."]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Limitations "}),T.jsx("br",{}),"Despite its benefits, CAVE does have drawbacks. First and foremost, it is generally expensive to set up and maintain, requiring specialized hardware like projectors or LED screens, trackers, high-end computers, and speakers. At the same time, it demands a large, dedicated space and is not portable, limiting its use to fixed locations. Moreover, the system is relatively complex, requiring trained personnel and frequent checkup and calibration. This also leads to heavy reliance on vendors for support, troubleshooting, and development of custom programs.",T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("br",{}),"In terms of VR experience, viewing is usually optimal for only one main user standing at the center, whose movements are tracked; while others may see distorted images, leading to confusion and even motion sickness. This can affect group engagement if not managed well."]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Major Applications"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"CAVE shines in training that requires real-life equipment rather than controllers or hand tracking. For instance, simulations involving fire extinguishers, like fire safety training, or steering wheels and control panels, like driver and pilot training, allow hands-on practice in a safe virtual setting. Emphasizing group experiences over individual ones, it is also suitable for virtual field trips, arts explorations, or collaborative projects where students discuss and interact together."]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Operation Modes"}),T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"1. 3D and 2D modes"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"CAVE can operate in 3D or 2D modes. In 3D mode, users wear active shutter glasses to view stereoscopic images displayed on the screens, creating a sense of depth and distance and enhancing immersion. In 2D mode, no glasses are needed, allowing simpler and faster setup with the expense of lowered sense of presence.",T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("br",{}),T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"2. Dynamic and fixed viewpoint"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"The viewpoint shown on the screens can either follow the user’s head position or be fixed. For dynamic viewpoint, the images are always in correct perspectives for the tracker user. However, this could lead to rapidly changing images for other users, potentially causing distortion and even motion sickness. Therefore, fixed viewpoint can be considered when accommodating multiple users."]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Key Takeaways "}),T.jsx("br",{}),"To compare CAVE with traditional headset VR, consider the following table:"]}),T.jsx("div",{className:"overflow-x-auto mt-6 mb-10",children:T.jsxs("table",{className:"w-full border-collapse border border-gray-300 text-[15px] text-gray-700",children:[T.jsx("thead",{children:T.jsxs("tr",{className:"bg-gray-100",children:[T.jsx("th",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 w-1/5"}),T.jsx("th",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 text-left font-bold w-2/5",children:"CAVE System"}),T.jsx("th",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 text-left font-bold w-2/5",children:"VR Headset"})]})}),T.jsxs("tbody",{children:[T.jsxs("tr",{children:[T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 font-bold align-top",children:"Setup"}),T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 align-top",children:"Projector or LED screens surrounding the user, motion tracking system, surround sound system"}),T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 align-top",children:"Head-mounted display with motion controllers or hand tracking"})]}),T.jsxs("tr",{children:[T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 font-bold align-top",children:"Cost"}),T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 align-top",children:"Varied depending on display system, tracking system, size, etc., but high installation and maintenance cost in general"}),T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 align-top",children:"Relatively low"})]}),T.jsxs("tr",{children:[T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 font-bold align-top",children:"Benefits"}),T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 align-top",children:"Shared experience, natural movement and interaction, possibility of incorporating real-life objects, ease of guidance"}),T.jsx("td",{className:"border border-gray-300 p-3 align-top",children:"Portable, small space requirement"})]})]})]})}),T.jsx("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-2 text-justify justify-last",children:"In summary, while HMD-based VR is accessible and individual-focused, CAVE offers a collaborative, integrated approach that could benefit educational activities, opening doors for more inclusive and practical applications. By addressing isolation and enabling real-world integration, CAVE can make learning more engaging for groups. And as technology evolves, hybrids of CAVE and headset VR may provide even more options."})]}),T.jsx("div",{className:"max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-4 mb-20 border-t border-gray-200 pt-10",children:T.jsx("div",{className:"flex justify-end items-start text-[0.915rem] text-slate-800",children:T.jsx(si,{to:"/blog",onClick:()=>window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:"instant"}),className:"oxanium-regular w-40 text-center border border-slate-400 rounded-4xl px-6 py-3 bg-white hover:bg-slate-50 transition font-medium",children:"Back"})})})]})}const vie=`
