`,Sz="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/Gyla7NnXMAAXSo_?format=jpg&name=large";function yie(){return T.jsxs("div",{className:"col-start-3 row-start-3 p-8 text-left",children:[T.jsxs("div",{className:"max-w-5xl mx-auto",children:[T.jsx("div",{className:"text-center text-sm font-medium text-gray-400 tracking-wide mb-3",children:"ELITE / Blog /"}),T.jsx("h1",{className:"text-center text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-wide uppercase text-slate-900",children:"Tech Unpacked: From Real-Life Objects to 3D Digital Twins"})]}),T.jsx("div",{className:"mt-10 -mx-8",children:T.jsx("img",{src:Sz,alt:"Blog Page 2",className:"w-full h-[420px] md:h-[520px] object-cover"})}),T.jsxs("div",{className:"max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-12 mb-20",children:[T.jsxs("div",{className:"flex justify-between items-center text-gray-600 text-sm mb-10",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"AUTHOR"}),T.jsx("span",{className:"uppercase tracking-wide",children:"12 December 2025"})]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Why Digital Twins? "}),T.jsx("br",{}),"As educators, we are often required to illustrate abstract ideas for our students. In fields like biology, history, or art, using physical objects can facilitate the linkage between theory and the real world. Unfortunately, time limits or practical issues can sometimes get in the way. Have you ever wanted to pass around an object in class, only to find there is not enough time for everyone to see it up close? Or maybe it is too valuable or fragile that even bringing it to class will be risky? Or have you spotted an interesting structure outside the classroom that could support your teaching, but struggled to show all its details using just photos?"]}),T.jsx("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:"This is where 3D digital twins can help. These are virtual 3D copies of real-life objects that allow flexible access, inspection and manipulation without physically involving the originals. [Image of student interacting with a 3D digital twin of a historical artifact on a tablet] They can be seamlessly integrated into teaching while bypassing the time and risk factors. For example, 3D twins of human organ specimens can give biology students interactive views of inner structures. Similarly, historical artifacts or artistic sculptures can be digitized for close study in art or archaeology classes. The real value of digital twins shows when the real-life objects are limited, valuable, or fragile. In a large lecture, not everyone can inspect a special sample closely, but a shared digital model offers equal access. For one-of-a-kind pieces, like ancient relics, digitization reduces the risk of them being damaged or lost during transportation."}),T.jsx("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:"These models can also act as assets in interactive courseware like games, virtual reality (VR) or augmented reality (AR) simulations, especially when the objects are so complicated that manual 3D modelling would be costly. In the virtual environment, students could interact with digital replicas just like in real life while being able to experiment safely and repeatedly."}),T.jsx("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:"Considering these advantages, one might assume that creating such digital twins requires significant financial investment. Luckily, the process has become remarkably accessible, rapid and economical in recent years with the help of technology. Here, we will cover two key methods: photogrammetry and artificial intelligence (AI)-based 3D model generation."}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Method 1: Photogrammetry"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"Photogrammetry is a method of 3D scanning that relies on images rather than depth data collected with specialized devices, eliminating the need for a dedicated 3D scanner while delivering full-color, highly detailed, and accurate results. It works by capturing multiple photos from various angles overlapping with one another, which the software program analyzes to determine the object's shape and construct a textured 3D model. The best part is its simplicity, requiring no fancy equipment — all you need to do is to pick up a smartphone or camera, walk around the object and snap pictures. The rest is handled by software."]}),T.jsx("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:"That said, certain setups can speed up the photo taking process substantially. For small objects, we can set up a turntable: place the item on it, rotate it, and take photos using a camera fixed on a tripod. [Image of photogrammetry setup with a lightbox and turntable] Additionally, we can enclose the turntable and the object in a lightbox to manipulate lighting and reduce shadows for improved results. For advanced needs, like full-body scans, rings of cameras can be installed in a small, circular room. By connecting all the cameras to a computer, the required images can be taken with the press of a button."}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:["There are a vast number of options when it comes to software tools for photogrammetry available on the market. Some combine photo capture and model generation into one package, including mobile apps like"," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://poly.cam/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"Polycam"}),","," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://www.realityscan.com/zh-CN",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"RealityScan"})," ","and"," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://www.kiriengine.app/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"KiriEngine"}),". They usually give on-screen tips, like where more photos are needed, making the workflow more beginner friendly. Due to the limitation in processing power of a mobile device, they usually send data to the cloud for model generation."]}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:["Once you become comfortable with taking photos without hints, computer software programs focusing on data processing and model generation are great options too. They work locally, provide much more control, and are usually more cost-effective! Options like"," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://www.agisoft.com/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"Metashape"}),","," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://www.3dflow.net/3df-zephyr-photogrammetry-software/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"3DF Zephyr"}),", and open-source"," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://alicevision.org/#meshroom",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"Meshroom"})," ","can all deliver strong results."]}),T.jsx("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-4 text-justify justify-last",children:"To save you some time and effort, here are some tips on taking photos for photogrammetry:"}),T.jsxs("ul",{className:"list-disc pl-10 space-y-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-8 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("li",{className:"pl-2",children:"For best results with objects that you can manipulate, shoot in at least three positions, which are usually upright (0 degrees), sideways (90 degrees), and upside down (180 degrees). The sideways set is especially important as it helps the software programs connect the other two sets. Also, take photos from different heights, like eye level and overhead."}),T.jsx("li",{className:"pl-2",children:"Use a smaller aperture (higher f-stop) to keep the whole object in sharp focus."}),T.jsx("li",{className:"pl-2",children:"Ensure even and soft lighting to show details and avoid harsh shadows. Remember to add front lighting when using a lightbox setup."}),T.jsx("li",{className:"pl-2",children:"If the object cannot stay steady in some positions, use supports like adhesive putty (e.g., Blu Tack) or stands. Most photogrammetry software programs allow removal of the areas around the supports to avoid artifacts."}),T.jsx("li",{className:"pl-2",children:"The number of images needed varies depending on the object's complexity, but typically, you will need from 80 up to 300 for optimal results. On the other hand, having too many images will lead to slower processing."}),T.jsx("li",{className:"pl-2",children:"Shiny or clear objects will cause issues; try a temporary matte coating, like powder, in situations where it is safe for the object."})]}),T.jsx("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:"Photogrammetry works well for detailed tasks, but sometimes you cannot spend much time with the object—like in a museum with restrictions or with something large like a building. Or maybe all you have about that object are several photos, like a historic structure. That is when AI 3D model generation steps in."}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Method 2: AI-Based 3D Model Generation"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"Tools like"," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://microsoft.github.io/TRELLIS.2/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"TRELLIS.2"}),","," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://3d-models.hunyuan.tencent.com/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"Hunyuan3D"}),", and"," ",T.jsx("a",{href:"https://www.meshy.ai/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",className:"text-blue-600 hover:underline",children:"Meshy"})," ","need just one or a few photos (possibly front, side, back, top) to estimate and create a 3D model. You can even start with text descriptions, though photos lead to much more predictable outcomes. [Image of AI software generating a 3D mesh from a single photograph] The AI model fills in gaps based on patterns it has learned from huge datasets, producing a model within minutes."]}),T.jsx("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:"Results will definitely not be perfect—there could be all kinds of distortions and errors, but the benefit is that these tools allow you to try multiple times until you are satisfied. And now is just the beginning of AI-based 3D model generation. As the technology improves, this will only become more accurate and thus useful for education."}),T.jsxs("p",{className:"text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700 mb-6 text-justify justify-last",children:[T.jsx("span",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Key Takeaway"}),T.jsx("br",{}),"By adding 3D digital twins to your teaching tools, you can increase student engagement and access. Whether using photogrammetry for accuracy or AI for speed, these methods help bring the real world into the classroom in new ways. Amazingly, tools like RealityScan are free to use, and you can download and try it out today. Maybe start with a simple object from your desk—the results might surprise you!"]})]}),T.jsx("div",{className:"max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-4 mb-20 border-t border-gray-200 pt-10",children:T.jsx("div",{className:"flex justify-end items-start text-[0.915rem] text-slate-800",children:T.jsx(si,{to:"/blog",onClick:()=>window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:"instant"}),className:"oxanium-regular w-40 text-center border border-slate-400 rounded-4xl px-6 py-3 bg-white hover:bg-slate-50 transition font-medium",children:"Back"})})})]})}const xie=`
